---Provides the configuration information to connect to Confluence as your data source.
---@class aws.kendra.ConfluenceConfiguration
---@field ServerUrl aws.kendra.Url The URL of your Confluence instance. Use the full URL of the server. For example, https://server.example.com:port/. You can also use an IP address, for example, https://192.168.1.113/.
---@field SecretArn aws.kendra.SecretArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an Secrets Manager secret that contains the user name and password required to connect to the Confluence instance. If you use Confluence Cloud, you use a generated API token as the password.
---@field Version aws.kendra.ConfluenceVersion|string The version or the type of Confluence installation to connect to.
---@field SpaceConfiguration? aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceConfiguration Configuration information for indexing Confluence spaces.
---@field PageConfiguration? aws.kendra.ConfluencePageConfiguration Configuration information for indexing Confluence pages.
---@field BlogConfiguration? aws.kendra.ConfluenceBlogConfiguration Configuration information for indexing Confluence blogs.
---@field AttachmentConfiguration? aws.kendra.ConfluenceAttachmentConfiguration Configuration information for indexing attachments to Confluence blogs and pages.
---@field VpcConfiguration? aws.kendra.DataSourceVpcConfiguration Configuration information for an Amazon Virtual Private Cloud to connect to your Confluence. For more information, see Configuring a VPC.
---@field InclusionPatterns? aws.kendra.DataSourceInclusionsExclusionsStrings A list of regular expression patterns to include certain blog posts, pages, spaces, or attachments in your Confluence. Content that matches the patterns are included in the index. Content that doesn't match the patterns is excluded from the index. If content matches both an inclusion and exclusion pattern, the exclusion pattern takes precedence and the content isn't included in the index.
---@field ExclusionPatterns? aws.kendra.DataSourceInclusionsExclusionsStrings A list of regular expression patterns to exclude certain blog posts, pages, spaces, or attachments in your Confluence. Content that matches the patterns are excluded from the index. Content that doesn't match the patterns is included in the index. If content matches both an inclusion and exclusion pattern, the exclusion pattern takes precedence and the content isn't included in the index.
---@field ProxyConfiguration? aws.kendra.ProxyConfiguration Configuration information to connect to your Confluence URL instance via a web proxy. You can use this option for Confluence Server.
---@field AuthenticationType? aws.kendra.ConfluenceAuthenticationType|string Whether you want to connect to Confluence using basic authentication of user name and password, or a personal access token. You can use a personal access token for Confluence Server.

---Configuration of the page settings for the Confluence data source.
---@class aws.kendra.ConfluencePageConfiguration
---@field PageFieldMappings? aws.kendra.ConfluencePageFieldMappingsList Maps attributes or field names of Confluence pages to Amazon Kendra index field names. To create custom fields, use the UpdateIndex API before you map to Confluence fields. For more information, see Mapping data source fields. The Confluence data source field names must exist in your Confluence custom metadata.

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluencePageFieldMappingsList aws.kendra.ConfluencePageToIndexFieldMapping[]

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluencePageFieldName "AUTHOR"|"CONTENT_STATUS"|"CREATED_DATE"|"DISPLAY_URL"|"ITEM_TYPE"|"LABELS"|"MODIFIED_DATE"|"PARENT_ID"|"SPACE_KEY"|"SPACE_NAME"|"URL"|"VERSION"

---Maps attributes or field names of Confluence pages to Amazon Kendra index field names. To create custom fields, use the UpdateIndex API before you map to Confluence fields. For more information, see Mapping data source fields. The Confluence data source field names must exist in your Confluence custom metadata.
---@class aws.kendra.ConfluencePageToIndexFieldMapping
---@field DataSourceFieldName? aws.kendra.ConfluencePageFieldName|string The name of the field in the data source.
---@field DateFieldFormat? aws.kendra.DataSourceDateFieldFormat The format for date fields in the data source. If the field specified in DataSourceFieldName is a date field you must specify the date format. If the field is not a date field, an exception is thrown.
---@field IndexFieldName? aws.kendra.IndexFieldName The name of the index field to map to the Confluence data source field. The index field type must match the Confluence field type.

---Configuration information for indexing Confluence spaces.
---@class aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceConfiguration
---@field CrawlPersonalSpaces? aws.kendra.Boolean TRUE to index personal spaces. You can add restrictions to items in personal spaces. If personal spaces are indexed, queries without user context information may return restricted items from a personal space in their results. For more information, see Filtering on user context.
---@field CrawlArchivedSpaces? aws.kendra.Boolean TRUE to index archived spaces.
---@field IncludeSpaces? aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceList A list of space keys for Confluence spaces. If you include a key, the blogs, documents, and attachments in the space are indexed. Spaces that aren't in the list aren't indexed. A space in the list must exist. Otherwise, Amazon Kendra logs an error when the data source is synchronized. If a space is in both the IncludeSpaces and the ExcludeSpaces list, the space is excluded.
---@field ExcludeSpaces? aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceList A list of space keys of Confluence spaces. If you include a key, the blogs, documents, and attachments in the space are not indexed. If a space is in both the ExcludeSpaces and the IncludeSpaces list, the space is excluded.
---@field SpaceFieldMappings? aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceFieldMappingsList Maps attributes or field names of Confluence spaces to Amazon Kendra index field names. To create custom fields, use the UpdateIndex API before you map to Confluence fields. For more information, see Mapping data source fields. The Confluence data source field names must exist in your Confluence custom metadata.

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceFieldMappingsList aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceToIndexFieldMapping[]

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceFieldName "DISPLAY_URL"|"ITEM_TYPE"|"SPACE_KEY"|"URL"

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceIdentifier string

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceList aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceIdentifier[]

---Maps attributes or field names of Confluence spaces to Amazon Kendra index field names. To create custom fields, use the UpdateIndex API before you map to Confluence fields. For more information, see Mapping data source fields. The Confluence data source field names must exist in your Confluence custom metadata.
---@class aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceToIndexFieldMapping
---@field DataSourceFieldName? aws.kendra.ConfluenceSpaceFieldName|string The name of the field in the data source.
---@field DateFieldFormat? aws.kendra.DataSourceDateFieldFormat The format for date fields in the data source. If the field specified in DataSourceFieldName is a date field you must specify the date format. If the field is not a date field, an exception is thrown.
---@field IndexFieldName? aws.kendra.IndexFieldName The name of the index field to map to the Confluence data source field. The index field type must match the Confluence field type.

---@alias aws.kendra.ConfluenceVersion "CLOUD"|"SERVER"

---Provides the configuration information that's required to connect to a database.
---@class aws.kendra.ConnectionConfiguration
---@field DatabaseHost aws.kendra.DatabaseHost The name of the host for the database. Can be either a string (host.subdomain.domain.tld) or an IPv4 or IPv6 address.
---@field DatabasePort aws.kendra.DatabasePort The port that the database uses for connections.
---@field DatabaseName aws.kendra.DatabaseName The name of the database containing the document data.
---@field TableName aws.kendra.TableName The name of the table that contains the document data.
---@field SecretArn aws.kendra.SecretArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an Secrets Manager secret that stores the credentials. The credentials should be a user-password pair. For more information, see Using a Database Data Source. For more information about Secrets Manager, see What Is Secrets Manager in the Secrets Manager user guide.

---@alias aws.kendra.Content string

---Provides the configuration information for your content sources, such as data sources, FAQs, and content indexed directly via BatchPutDocument.
---@class aws.kendra.ContentSourceConfiguration
---@field DataSourceIds? aws.kendra.DataSourceIdList The identifier of the data sources you want to use for your Amazon Kendra experience.
---@field FaqIds? aws.kendra.FaqIdsList The identifier of the FAQs that you want to use for your Amazon Kendra experience.
---@field DirectPutContent? aws.kendra.Boolean TRUE to use documents you indexed directly using the BatchPutDocument API.

---@alias aws.kendra.ContentType "PDF"|"HTML"|"MS_WORD"|"PLAIN_TEXT"|"PPT"|"RTF"|"XML"|"XSLT"|"MS_EXCEL"|"CSV"|"JSON"|"MD"

---A corrected misspelled word in a query.
---@class aws.kendra.Correction
---@field BeginOffset? aws.kendra.Integer The zero-based location in the response string or text where the corrected word starts.
---@field EndOffset? aws.kendra.Integer The zero-based location in the response string or text where the corrected word ends.
---@field Term? aws.kendra.String The string or text of a misspelled word in a query.
---@field CorrectedTerm? aws.kendra.String The string or text of a corrected misspelled word in a query.

---@alias aws.kendra.CorrectionList aws.kendra.Correction[]

---@alias aws.kendra.CrawlDepth number

---@class aws.kendra.CreateAccessControlConfigurationRequest
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index to create an access control configuration for your documents.
---@field Name aws.kendra.AccessControlConfigurationName A name for the access control configuration.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for the access control configuration.
---@field AccessControlList? aws.kendra.PrincipalList Information on principals (users and/or groups) and which documents they should have access to. This is useful for user context filtering, where search results are filtered based on the user or their group access to documents.
---@field HierarchicalAccessControlList? aws.kendra.HierarchicalPrincipalList The list of principal lists that define the hierarchy for which documents users should have access to.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create an access control configuration. Multiple calls to the CreateAccessControlConfiguration API with the same client token will create only one access control configuration.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateAccessControlConfigurationResponse
---@field Id aws.kendra.AccessControlConfigurationId The identifier of the access control configuration for your documents in an index.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateDataSourceRequest
---@field Name aws.kendra.DataSourceName A name for the data source connector.
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index you want to use with the data source connector.
---@field Type aws.kendra.DataSourceType|string The type of data source repository. For example, SHAREPOINT.
---@field Configuration? aws.kendra.DataSourceConfiguration Configuration information to connect to your data source repository.
---@field VpcConfiguration? aws.kendra.DataSourceVpcConfiguration Configuration information for an Amazon Virtual Private Cloud to connect to your data source. For more information, see Configuring a VPC.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for the data source connector.
---@field Schedule? aws.kendra.ScanSchedule Sets the frequency for Amazon Kendra to check the documents in your data source repository and update the index. If you don't set a schedule Amazon Kendra will not periodically update the index. You can call the StartDataSourceSyncJob API to update the index.
---@field RoleArn? aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to access the data source and required resources. For more information, see IAM access roles for Amazon Kendra..
---@field Tags? aws.kendra.TagList A list of key-value pairs that identify or categorize the data source connector. You can also use tags to help control access to the data source connector. Tag keys and values can consist of Unicode letters, digits, white space, and any of the following symbols: _ . : / = + - @.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create a data source connector. Multiple calls to the CreateDataSource API with the same client token will create only one data source connector.
---@field LanguageCode? aws.kendra.LanguageCode The code for a language. This allows you to support a language for all documents when creating the data source connector. English is supported by default. For more information on supported languages, including their codes, see Adding documents in languages other than English.
---@field CustomDocumentEnrichmentConfiguration? aws.kendra.CustomDocumentEnrichmentConfiguration Configuration information for altering document metadata and content during the document ingestion process.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateDataSourceResponse
---@field Id aws.kendra.DataSourceId The identifier of the data source connector.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateExperienceRequest
---@field Name aws.kendra.ExperienceName A name for your Amazon Kendra experience.
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index for your Amazon Kendra experience.
---@field RoleArn? aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to access Query API, GetQuerySuggestions API, and other required APIs. The role also must include permission to access IAM Identity Center that stores your user and group information. For more information, see IAM access roles for Amazon Kendra.
---@field Configuration? aws.kendra.ExperienceConfiguration Configuration information for your Amazon Kendra experience. This includes ContentSourceConfiguration, which specifies the data source IDs and/or FAQ IDs, and UserIdentityConfiguration, which specifies the user or group information to grant access to your Amazon Kendra experience.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for your Amazon Kendra experience.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create your Amazon Kendra experience. Multiple calls to the CreateExperience API with the same client token creates only one Amazon Kendra experience.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateExperienceResponse
---@field Id aws.kendra.ExperienceId The identifier of your Amazon Kendra experience.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateFaqRequest
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index for the FAQ.
---@field Name aws.kendra.FaqName A name for the FAQ.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for the FAQ.
---@field S3Path aws.kendra.S3Path The path to the FAQ file in S3.
---@field RoleArn aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to access the S3 bucket that contains the FAQ file. For more information, see IAM access roles for Amazon Kendra.
---@field Tags? aws.kendra.TagList A list of key-value pairs that identify the FAQ. You can use the tags to identify and organize your resources and to control access to resources.
---@field FileFormat? aws.kendra.FaqFileFormat|string The format of the FAQ input file. You can choose between a basic CSV format, a CSV format that includes customs attributes in a header, and a JSON format that includes custom attributes.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create a FAQ. Multiple calls to the CreateFaqRequest API with the same client token will create only one FAQ.
---@field LanguageCode? aws.kendra.LanguageCode The code for a language. This allows you to support a language for the FAQ document. English is supported by default. For more information on supported languages, including their codes, see Adding documents in languages other than English.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateFaqResponse
---@field Id? aws.kendra.FaqId The identifier of the FAQ.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateFeaturedResultsSetRequest
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index that you want to use for featuring results.
---@field FeaturedResultsSetName aws.kendra.FeaturedResultsSetName A name for the set of featured results.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.FeaturedResultsSetDescription A description for the set of featured results.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create a set of featured results. Multiple calls to the CreateFeaturedResultsSet API with the same client token will create only one featured results set.
---@field Status? aws.kendra.FeaturedResultsSetStatus|string The current status of the set of featured results. When the value is ACTIVE, featured results are ready for use. You can still configure your settings before setting the status to ACTIVE. You can set the status to ACTIVE or INACTIVE using the UpdateFeaturedResultsSet API. The queries you specify for featured results must be unique per featured results set for each index, whether the status is ACTI...
---@field QueryTexts? aws.kendra.QueryTextList A list of queries for featuring results. For more information on the list of queries, see FeaturedResultsSet.
---@field FeaturedDocuments? aws.kendra.FeaturedDocumentList A list of document IDs for the documents you want to feature at the top of the search results page. For more information on the list of documents, see FeaturedResultsSet.
---@field Tags? aws.kendra.TagList A list of key-value pairs that identify or categorize the featured results set. You can also use tags to help control access to the featured results set. Tag keys and values can consist of Unicode letters, digits, white space, and any of the following symbols:_ . : / = + - @.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateFeaturedResultsSetResponse
---@field FeaturedResultsSet? aws.kendra.FeaturedResultsSet Information on the set of featured results. This includes the identifier of the featured results set, whether the featured results set is active or inactive, when the featured results set was created, and more.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateIndexRequest
---@field Name aws.kendra.IndexName A name for the index.
---@field Edition? aws.kendra.IndexEdition|string The Amazon Kendra edition to use for the index. Choose DEVELOPER_EDITION for indexes intended for development, testing, or proof of concept. Use ENTERPRISE_EDITION for production. Use GEN_AI_ENTERPRISE_EDITION for creating generative AI applications. Once you set the edition for an index, it can't be changed.
---@field RoleArn aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to access your Amazon CloudWatch logs and metrics. For more information, see IAM access roles for Amazon Kendra.
---@field ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration? aws.kendra.ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration The identifier of the KMS customer managed key (CMK) that's used to encrypt data indexed by Amazon Kendra. Amazon Kendra doesn't support asymmetric CMKs.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for the index.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create an index. Multiple calls to the CreateIndex API with the same client token will create only one index.
---@field Tags? aws.kendra.TagList A list of key-value pairs that identify or categorize the index. You can also use tags to help control access to the index. Tag keys and values can consist of Unicode letters, digits, white space, and any of the following symbols: _ . : / = + - @.
---@field UserTokenConfigurations? aws.kendra.UserTokenConfigurationList The user token configuration.
---@field UserContextPolicy? aws.kendra.UserContextPolicy|string The user context policy.
---@field UserGroupResolutionConfiguration? aws.kendra.UserGroupResolutionConfiguration Gets users and groups from IAM Identity Center identity source. To configure this, see UserGroupResolutionConfiguration. This is useful for user context filtering, where search results are filtered based on the user or their group access to documents.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateIndexResponse
---@field Id? aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index. Use this identifier when you query an index, set up a data source, or index a document.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateQuerySuggestionsBlockListRequest
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index you want to create a query suggestions block list for.
---@field Name aws.kendra.QuerySuggestionsBlockListName A name for the block list.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for the block list.
---@field SourceS3Path aws.kendra.S3Path The S3 path to your block list text file in your S3 bucket.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create a query suggestions block list.
---@field RoleArn aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to access your S3 bucket that contains the block list text file. For more information, see IAM access roles for Amazon Kendra.
---@field Tags? aws.kendra.TagList A list of key-value pairs that identify or categorize the block list. Tag keys and values can consist of Unicode letters, digits, white space, and any of the following symbols: _ . : / = + - @.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateQuerySuggestionsBlockListResponse
---@field Id? aws.kendra.QuerySuggestionsBlockListId The identifier of the block list.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateThesaurusRequest
---@field IndexId aws.kendra.IndexId The identifier of the index for the thesaurus.
---@field Name aws.kendra.ThesaurusName A name for the thesaurus.
---@field Description? aws.kendra.Description A description for the thesaurus.
---@field RoleArn aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to access your S3 bucket that contains the thesaurus file. For more information, see IAM access roles for Amazon Kendra.
---@field Tags? aws.kendra.TagList A list of key-value pairs that identify or categorize the thesaurus. You can also use tags to help control access to the thesaurus. Tag keys and values can consist of Unicode letters, digits, white space, and any of the following symbols: _ . : / = + - @.
---@field SourceS3Path aws.kendra.S3Path The path to the thesaurus file in S3.
---@field ClientToken? aws.kendra.ClientTokenName A token that you provide to identify the request to create a thesaurus. Multiple calls to the CreateThesaurus API with the same client token will create only one thesaurus.

---@class aws.kendra.CreateThesaurusResponse
---@field Id? aws.kendra.ThesaurusId The identifier of the thesaurus.

---Provides the configuration information for altering document metadata and content during the document ingestion process.
---@class aws.kendra.CustomDocumentEnrichmentConfiguration
---@field InlineConfigurations? aws.kendra.InlineCustomDocumentEnrichmentConfigurationList Configuration information to alter document attributes or metadata fields and content when ingesting documents into Amazon Kendra.
---@field PreExtractionHookConfiguration? aws.kendra.HookConfiguration Configuration information for invoking a Lambda function in Lambda on the original or raw documents before extracting their metadata and text. You can use a Lambda function to apply advanced logic for creating, modifying, or deleting document metadata and content. For more information, see Advanced data manipulation.
---@field PostExtractionHookConfiguration? aws.kendra.HookConfiguration Configuration information for invoking a Lambda function in Lambda on the structured documents with their metadata and text extracted. You can use a Lambda function to apply advanced logic for creating, modifying, or deleting document metadata and content. For more information, see Advanced data manipulation.
---@field RoleArn? aws.kendra.RoleArn The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an IAM role with permission to run PreExtractionHookConfiguration and PostExtractionHookConfiguration for altering document metadata and content during the document ingestion process. For more information, see an IAM roles for Amazon Kendra.

